    "strategy": {
      "summary": "ComfortDelGro's 2023 decarbonization strategy centers on a comprehensive transition of its global transportation fleet toward cleaner energy solutions while optimizing operations to reduce emissions. The company has committed to science-based emissions reduction targets approved by the Science Based Targets initiative (SBTi) in 2022, aligning with the goal of limiting global warming to 1.5°C above pre-industrial levels. Their primary focus is transitioning vehicle fleets away from traditional internal combustion engines (ICE) to cleaner alternatives, with hybrids and EVs already accounting for 48% of their global vehicle fleet. The company has established concrete targets to transition 90% of their car fleet and 50% of their bus fleet to cleaner energy vehicles by 2030, with specific milestones for phasing out ICE vehicles. Their strategy includes implementing a decarbonization plan that replaces traditional ICE vehicles with hybrid, electric, and hydrogen vehicles, while ensuring the majority of their existing ICE fleet meets Euro 5 or higher emission standards to reduce harmful exhaust emissions.\n\nThe company is executing this strategy through multiple specific initiatives across their global operations. In Singapore, they operate a fully electric fleet of shuttle buses, serving as the largest electric private bus operator in the country with 31 operational e-buses across National University of Singapore (NUS) campuses and 18 e-buses across Nanyang Technological University (NTU) campuses. They've formed strategic partnerships, including a joint venture with Guangzhou Public Transport Group for EV charging infrastructure and launched electric shuttle buses in local universities. ComfortDelGro has also expanded its cleaner energy fleet in the UK, where Metrolines has become the country's biggest operator of hybrid buses. Additionally, the company is implementing energy management practices across operations, including electrification of fleets, process improvements, and investment in renewable energy opportunities such as solar power.\n\nTo support their decarbonization journey, ComfortDelGro has established robust governance and monitoring mechanisms. They published their second Taskforce for Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) report in December 2023, which identifies climate-related physical and transition risks and opportunities relevant to their business operations. The company has developed business continuity plans (BCPs) to address physical climate hazards and is creating a comprehensive Climate Transition Plan for implementation in the coming years. They utilize scenario analysis to understand climate risks and opportunities, with specific focus on carbon pricing, changing consumer expectations, and regulatory requirements. The company also employs telematics systems on their electric buses to monitor driving patterns and optimize operations for maximum efficiency, while regularly reviewing and updating their fleet transition plan to account for technological advances and regulatory requirements.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across several images. Strategy focuses primarily on operational emissions (fleet transition) with specific targets and initiatives. The company has concrete implementation details with measurable targets, specific partnerships, and technological implementations. Strategy integrates both short-term actions (current fleet composition) and long-term planning (2030 and 2050 targets).",
      "status": null
    },
