      "summary": "Great Eastern's decarbonization strategy for 2023 centers on a multi-pronged approach to achieve Net Zero by 2050, with specific interim targets and concrete actions across investment portfolios, operations, and product development. The company has established a Sustainability Taskforce with two key objectives: meeting Net Zero targets and addressing key ESG considerations. This taskforce focuses on four strategic areas including Net Zero, Product Development, Community and Culture, and Regulatory/Risk Management to guide their decarbonization journey.\n\nThe company has implemented specific, measurable initiatives to reduce emissions across their investment portfolios, having successfully reduced the carbon footprint of their listed equity portfolio by 22.2% and corporate bond portfolio by 40.5% in 2023 (baseline year: 2020). They have transited S$200mn of developed markets listed equity portfolio to be managed with a climate mandate and added close to S$900mn in green bonds. For operational emissions, they reduced total operational emissions intensity by 25% (baseline year: 2019) and are on track to meet their 30% reduction target by 2025 through infrastructure improvement projects. The company tracks their carbon footprint using the Global GHG Protocol, with the carbon footprint dashboard reported regularly at SC and RMC meetings.\n\nIn product development, Great Eastern launched the GREAT EV Protect in September 2023 to support the national shift toward electric vehicles, which covers both EV adoption and ancillary aspects like home content damage from EV-related fires. In Indonesia, they enhanced their House Holder/Property Insurance to include coverage for solar panels. They've also applied MSCI ESG Rating to their Investment Linked Products (ILP) fund curation process since 2022, with 73% of their ILP funds rated A and above as of December 2023. The company engages with the Asia Investor Group for Climate Change (AIGCC) to drive constructive conversations with investee companies about their transition plans, recognizing that decarbonization trajectory will not be linear due to balancing transition financing with other investment objectives.",
