    "strategy": {
      "summary": "ComfortDelGro's 2021 decarbonization strategy centers on a science-based approach aligned with the 1.5°C pathway, with fleet electrification as the cornerstone of their climate action. The company committed to the Science Based Targets initiative (SBTi) in February 2021, setting a clear direction to achieve the more ambitious 1.5°C scenario rather than the standard 'Well Below 2°C' target. Their strategic framework focuses on three key pillars: electrifying their fleet, improving operational efficiencies, and transitioning to clean, renewable energy sources. As the first Singaporean mobility operator to commit to SBTi and the 1.5°C business ambition, they established a Board-level Sustainability Committee to oversee integration of Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG) considerations into business strategy, recognizing their role in delivering clean, low-carbon transport solutions as an international mobility operator.\n\nThe company implemented concrete fleet transition initiatives across multiple geographies to reduce emissions. In Singapore, SBS Transit partnered with the Land Transport Authority to introduce 10 additional electric buses, increasing their total to 30, while ComfortDelGro Taxi targeted 1,000 electric taxis on the road by 2023. They also began operating fully electrified private shuttle bus fleets at the National University of Singapore and Nanyang Technological University campuses. To support this transition, ComfortDelGro Engineering-ENGIE consortium secured 479 out of 632 charging station tenders, marking the first step in bolstering local EV infrastructure. The company established a fleet transition plan aiming to halve Scope 1 and 2 emissions by 2032, while also setting an ambitious Scope 3 reduction target of over 60% to demonstrate commitment beyond regulatory requirements.\n\nComfortDelGro's strategy is supported by robust governance structures and monitoring systems. They aligned with the Task Force on Climate-Related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) to understand physical and transition risks, and implemented real-time data collection systems to monitor and measure fuel and energy usage. The company committed to annual reviews of their emission reduction performance to ensure alignment with science-based targets, and established a framework to consider high-quality carbon credits for hard-to-abate emissions. Additionally, they formed strategic partnerships with government agencies and technology providers to accelerate their decarbonization journey while maintaining operational efficiency and service quality across their global operations.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across several pages. Strategy focuses primarily on operational emissions (Scope 1 & 2) with specific fleet electrification targets and timelines. The company has established concrete targets for Scope 3 emissions (over 60% reduction) despite not being required under SBTi standards. Strategy includes both operational changes and supporting infrastructure investments with specific numerical targets. Implementation details are provided for multiple geographic operations with clear timelines.",
      "status": null
    },
