      "summary": "Meta's 2021 decarbonization strategy centers on a multi-pronged approach that combines direct carbon removal, science-based risk assessment, and strategic partnerships. The company prioritizes carbon removal projects that sequester equivalent CO2 emissions, with specific investments in Kenya and Mexico representing over 200,000 tons of carbon sequestration through forest and soil-based solutions. These projects follow strict criteria including third-party verification, climate justice considerations, and avoidance of adverse impacts elsewhere. Meta also conducts comprehensive climate risk assessments across more than 500 priority sites using IPCC Representative Concentration Pathways 4.5 and 8.5 scenarios to understand physical risks like wildfires, sea-level rise, and water stress, informing their operational strategy and resilience planning.\n\nTo accelerate broader decarbonization efforts, Meta launched the $1 million Climate Misinformation Grant in partnership with the International Fact-Checking Network to fund fact-checkers and solution providers combating false climate information. The company actively supports market development for emerging decarbonization technologies, including contributing to the Frontier advance market commitment to catalyze technological carbon removal credits and supporting the Sustainable Aviation Buyers Alliance (SABA) to drive demand for sustainable aviation fuel. Additionally, Meta conducts life cycle assessments of Reality Labs products to understand environmental impacts across the product life cycle and has committed to building the metaverse sustainably, with their net zero commitment explicitly including these new products. The Climate Science Center serves as a key resource, expanding access to science-based climate information across more than 150 countries with over 3.8 million followers.",
