      "summary": "The company’s 2021 decarbonization strategy is grounded in a risk-informed approach to align with national climate targets and manage transition and physical climate risks. PLife REIT conducted a qualitative environmental risk assessment under Net Zero (limiting global warming to 1.5°C) and Business-as-Usual (BAU, >4°C warming) scenarios, focusing on transition risks from regulatory, market, and technological shifts in a low-carbon economy and physical risks from climate impacts. Recognizing that approximately 60% of its portfolio consists of hospital buildings in Singapore, the strategy explicitly ties medium- and long-term decarbonization efforts to Singapore’s nationally determined contributions under the Paris Agreement: peaking emissions by 2030, halving 2030 peak emissions by 2050, and targeting net-zero emissions in the second half of the century. \n\nTo operationalize this alignment, the company assesses climate risks across short-term (by 2025), medium-term (by 2030), and long-term (by 2050) time horizons, which correspond to its capital planning and investment cycles. For its Singapore portfolio, the strategy prioritizes adherence to national emission milestones, while for its Japan portfolio, PLife REIT collaborates closely with operators to monitor and manage climate-related risks through ongoing engagement. The Net Zero scenario informs proactive adaptation to low-carbon economic shifts, whereas the BAU scenario drives physical risk mitigation using climate model data (CMIP5/CMIP6 for RCP 8.5 pathway). This structured approach ensures decarbonization efforts are embedded within financial and operational decision-making, emphasizing alignment with national frameworks and stakeholder collaboration to address evolving climate risks.",
