    "strategy": {
      "summary": "Hong Kong Land’s decarbonization strategy for 2022 is anchored in science-based targets aligned with the 1.5°C pathway, integrating climate action into core operations and asset management. The company committed to a 46.2% reduction in Scope 1 and 2 greenhouse gas emissions by 2030 (from 2019 levels) and a 22% reduction in carbon intensity for Scope 3 emissions over the same period, with these targets validated by the Science Based Targets initiative in June 2022. Central to the approach is accelerating green building certification and expanding renewable energy adoption across its portfolio to reduce operational emissions while addressing supply chain impacts through carbon intensity targets.\n\nThe company executed concrete initiatives to advance these goals: 88% of its leasing portfolio, including joint ventures, achieved green building certification by year-end 2022, with all buildings in Hong Kong and Singapore (51% of the portfolio) securing the highest possible ratings (BEAM Plus Platinum and Green Mark Platinum). A landmark project was the Beijing flagship WF CENTRAL, which became one of the first commercial complexes in the city powered entirely by renewable energy—sourced from photovoltaic and wind systems in Northwest China. Additionally, the strategy includes performance-based targets on embodied carbon emissions for construction materials like concrete, rebar, and structural steel, alongside consistent retrofitting of existing assets to enhance energy efficiency.\n\nTo ensure effective implementation, Hong Kong Land established robust governance and monitoring frameworks. It performs and updates climate risk assessments and adaptation plans in line with the Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures, while increasing renewable energy procurement and expanding onsite generation capacity. The company also aligns its disclosures with evolving ESG reporting standards and participates in sustainable financing markets to drive the financial sector’s green transition, embedding decarbonization into long-term risk management and business continuity processes.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across sustainability and risk management sections. Strategy focuses on operational emissions (Scope 1/2), green building certification, renewable energy projects, and Scope 3 carbon intensity. Specific initiatives include portfolio-wide green certification, project-specific renewable energy adoption, and embodied carbon targets for construction materials. Implementation details provided with measurable outcomes and project examples.",
      "status": null
    },
