    "strategy": {
      "summary": "Keppel's decarbonization strategy for 2022 centers on a dual approach: running its business sustainably while making sustainability the business through solutions that contribute to sustainable development. The company has committed to halving absolute Scope 1 and 2 carbon emissions by 2030 (compared to 2020 levels) and achieving net zero by 2050, aligned with the Paris Agreement's 1.5°C scenario. A key pillar is the transition to renewable energy, with specific targets to source 50% of electricity from renewables by 2025 and 100% by 2030. The company significantly expanded its renewables portfolio from 1.1 GW to 2.6 GW in 2022, advancing toward its 7 GW target by 2030 through projects like the 600 MW Sakra Cogen Plant in Singapore (hydrogen-ready, completion 2026) and Keppel Renewable Energy's 500+ MW solar project in Queensland, Australia.\n\nTo implement this strategy, Keppel has established specific initiatives across business units. Keppel Land, M1, and Keppel Infrastructure have set Science-Based Targets initiative (SBTi)-aligned emissions reduction targets, while Keppel Data Centres aims for net zero Scope 1 and 2 emissions for new data centre assets by 2030. The company is pioneering cross-border renewable energy trade between Laos, Thailand, and Malaysia, and developing innovative solutions including floating solar photovoltaic systems with the National University of Singapore and climate-resilient nearshore developments. Energy efficiency is addressed through high-efficiency upgrades like the one completed at Keppel Merlimau Cogen Plant in 2022, and the company is exploring green ammonia and hydrogen solutions with international partners.\n\nSupporting these initiatives is a robust governance framework established in 2022, including the Board Sustainability and Safety Committee (BSSC) that meets quarterly to oversee sustainability strategy. The company has implemented a shadow carbon price (SCP) of US$20 per tonne of CO2e (increasing to US$50/tCO2e over time) to evaluate new investment decisions, and conducts regular scenario planning to assess climate risks and opportunities. Keppel also leverages partnerships with organizations like KAIF, Greenko, and Singapore Land Authority to advance decarbonization efforts, while integrating sustainability into its risk management framework and performance metrics across all business units.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across TCFD Disclosures, Decarbonisation Strategy, and Climate Action sections. Strategy includes specific targets, business unit initiatives, and governance structures. Implementation details are provided for key projects with specific metrics and timelines.",
      "status": null
    },
