      "summary": "The company's decarbonization strategy for 2024 centers on operational energy efficiency across all facility types, with specific initiatives implemented at Headends, Data Centres, and Offices. The strategy focuses on replacing outdated equipment with energy-efficient alternatives, including air conditioning units, cooling systems, and lighting. They implement zone-based control of air conditioning and lighting to reduce power consumption, reconfigure equipment layouts to minimize cooling needs, and apply power saving modes to office equipment like computers and copy machines. The company has established a policy framework 'Guidelines for Electricity Usage in TBC Offices' to standardize energy reduction practices across all facilities.\n\nTo support this approach, the company conducts monthly monitoring of electricity bills to identify unusual consumption patterns and makes necessary adjustments. They also implement real-time monitoring of power consumption on panels for newly installed power distribution units and regularly assess actual demand to optimize electricity metering and contract capacity. These monitoring practices enable data-driven energy management decisions. The company has expanded its carbon disclosures to include Scope 1 emissions, which totaled 1,928 tCO₂e in 2024, with detailed breakdowns showing 620 tCO₂e from refrigerant gas use, 1,302 tCO₂e from company-owned vehicles, and 6 tCO₂e from power generators.\n\nThe company recognizes its role in contributing to global GHG reduction efforts and aligns with Taiwan's goal of achieving net zero emissions by 2050. While their 2024 strategy primarily focuses on energy efficiency measures, they have set a target to further enhance energy efficiency in 2025 with an Energy Use Intensity (EUI) of 0.670 MWh/m² or lower, building on the 2024 reduction from 0.648 to 0.619 MWh/m².",
