    "strategy": {
      "summary": "Keppel's decarbonization strategy for 2024 centers on a three-pillar approach: business transformation, sustainable operations, and integrating sustainability into core business functions. The company has established concrete targets to halve Scope 1 and 2 emissions by 2030 (compared to 2020 levels) and achieve net zero by 2050, with an 81.6% reduction already achieved in 2024. A primary focus is decarbonizing Singapore's power sector, where approximately 85% of their Scope 3 emissions originate, through strategic initiatives including importing renewable energy under the LTMS-PiP (Laos PDR-Thailand-Malaysia-Singapore Power Integration Project), importing 300 MW of solar power from Indonesia and 1 GW of low carbon electricity from Cambodia, and developing the hydrogen-compatible Keppel Sakra Cogen Plant (85% completed as of end-2024). The company is also actively transitioning its power generation portfolio with targets to reduce carbon emissions intensity from 0.366 tCO2/MWh in 2024 to 0.294 tCO2/MWh by 2030.\n\nKeppel's implementation strategy includes specific divisional targets and initiatives across its business units. The Real Estate Division aims to reduce Scope 3 emissions by 20% per square meter by 2030, while the Infrastructure Division targets reducing emissions intensity from ~0.37 tCO2/MWh to 0.27 tCO2/MWh by 2035. The company has set a renewable energy target of 50% of electricity use by 2025 (with 40.7% achieved in 2024) and 100% by 2030, while growing its renewable energy portfolio to 7 GW by 2030 (3.8 GW as of end-2024). Specific actions include divesting from oil-related assets, developing cost-effective CCUS technology, and phasing out coal-fired plants while expanding into renewables, green ammonia, and hydrogen.\n\nTo support these initiatives, Keppel has implemented robust governance structures with regular Board discussions on climate matters, third-party climate risk assessment tools, and ESG integration throughout its investment processes. The company actively engages with government initiatives and international organizations including the UN Global Compact, Singapore Low Carbon Network, and GRI Singapore. Keppel's REITs and business trusts have established specific emission reduction targets aligned with the 1.5°C pathway, with Keppel Infrastructure Trust targeting 2 GW of renewable capacity by 2030. The company also leverages its role as a global asset manager to integrate sustainability considerations across its investment lifecycle, avoiding investments in pollutive sectors while focusing on climate-resilient opportunities.",
      "notes": "Comprehensive multi-page strategy section found across images 3-10. Strategy focuses on both operational emissions reduction and supporting national decarbonization efforts in Singapore. Specific implementation details provided for power sector decarbonization, renewable energy expansion, and division-specific targets. Strategy includes concrete metrics, timelines, and technology investments rather than just high-level commitments.",
      "status": null
    },
