    "strategy": {
      "summary": "Singapore Airlines' decarbonization strategy for 2024 centers on a multi-pronged approach focused on sustainable aviation fuel (SAF) adoption, operational efficiency, and strategic partnerships to achieve its net zero carbon emissions target by 2050. The company has established concrete near-term targets, including replacing 5% of total fuel requirements with SAF by 2030 and achieving carbon neutral growth based on ICAO's CORSIA baseline starting from 2024. SIA is conducting comprehensive climate scenario analysis across short-term (2025), medium-term (2030), and long-term (2050) horizons to prepare for transition risks while aligning with Singapore Green Plan 2030 sustainability targets and the Group's 2030 SAF target.\n\nThe company has implemented several specific initiatives in 2024 to advance its decarbonization journey. In May 2024, SIA signed an agreement with Neste to purchase 1,000 tonnes of neat SAF for delivery to Singapore Changi Airport, marking the first batch to be produced in Neste's Singapore refinery. The airline is actively working with aviation ecosystem partners through the International Aviation Partners (IAP) to develop and implement the Sustainable Air Hub Blueprint for Singapore, launched in February 2024. SIA is also monitoring policy developments on SAF mandates globally, engaging with suppliers on demand updates, and participating in CAAS' consultation sessions regarding the SAF levy implementation. Additionally, the company is enhancing building sustainability by working toward BCA Green Mark accreditation for SIA-owned buildings by FY2026/27 and implementing energy reduction initiatives across its facilities.\n\nTo support these efforts, SIA is exploring financing mechanisms for eligible carbon offsets to meet emissions reporting obligations and is monitoring consumer preferences through regular market scans to develop improved products and services with lower resource footprints. The company has also committed to reducing cabin and catering food waste, minimizing packaging waste, and eliminating single-use plastics. SIA's strategy includes active collaboration with industry stakeholders through the Association of Asia Pacific Airlines (AAPA) collective ambition to achieve 5% SAF substitution by 2030, while simultaneously conducting climate scenario analysis to assess resilience across different emission pathways and time horizons.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across all provided images. Strategy focuses primarily on SAF adoption and operational efficiency with specific 2024 initiatives. Concrete actions include SAF procurement agreement with Neste, Sustainable Air Hub Blueprint implementation, and building sustainability measures. Strategy details specific targets and near-term actions rather than being solely high-level commitments.",
      "status": null
    },
