    "strategy": {
      "summary": "Straco Corporation Limited’s 2024 decarbonization strategy is structured around the TCFD framework, with a focus on four key pillars: strategy, governance, risk management, and metrics and targets. The company conducted its first qualitative climate scenario analysis (CSA) in FY2024, covering assets in China and Singapore, to assess climate-related risks and opportunities. This analysis, aligned with the Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) framework, informs strategic decisions to enhance resilience against evolving environmental challenges and prepares the company for Singapore’s upcoming sustainability reporting requirements under the ISSB standards. The CSA also reinforces Straco’s commitment to long-term value creation by identifying physical and transition risks across short (2030), medium (2050), and long-term (2100) horizons.\n\nTo address transition risks, Straco has implemented concrete initiatives targeting energy efficiency and resource optimization. These include adopting internal carbon pricing, setting science-based emissions targets, and executing operational upgrades such as replacing 287 lighting fixtures with LEDs, installing motion-sensor lighting in carparks, and integrating solar panels to reduce energy consumption. The company also pilots a rainwater collection system for landscaping irrigation by 2025 and adopts eco-friendly practices like paper-packed water and e-name cards to minimize waste. Additionally, Straco enhances energy efficiency through optimized air conditioning, energy management systems, and NEWater for irrigation, while participating in government carbon reduction policies to align with regulatory compliance.\n\nFor accountability, Straco tracks and discloses Scope 1 and 2 emissions (Scope 1: 43 → 86 tCO₂e; Scope 2: 8,241 → 8,604 tCO₂e from FY2023 to FY2024) and sets a target to maintain or reduce 2024 energy, GHG, and water consumption levels within a 10% variance. Governance is reinforced through TCFD-aligned disclosures, including board oversight of climate risks and management’s role in assessing and managing these risks, as detailed in the Sustainability Governance section. These measures collectively form a structured approach to embed climate resilience into core business operations while addressing regulatory and stakeholder expectations.",
      "notes": "Strategy detailed across multiple pages (25, 28, 31, 34), focuses primarily on operational emissions (Scope 1/2), includes specific initiatives with implementation details and metrics, and aligns with TCFD framework and ISSB standards.",
      "status": null
    },
