      "summary": "TNB's 2022 decarbonization strategy centers on a multi-faceted approach to transition Malaysia's energy sector toward net zero emissions by 2050. The company announced its Energy Transition (ET) Plan in August 2022, committing to significant Capital Expenditure (CAPEX) for initiatives that will reduce emissions intensity by 35% by 2035 while targeting a 50% reduction in coal generation capacity. TNB's strategy is structured around four key priorities: accelerating decarbonization through early coal plant retirement and cleaner technology adoption; expanding renewable energy portfolios both domestically and internationally; modernizing grid infrastructure for greater flexibility; and empowering customers through electrification initiatives. The company is implementing concrete actions including the early retirement of selected coal plants before Power Purchase Agreement expiration, feasibility studies for coal plant co-firing with ammonia and biomass, and the development of the 2.1 GW Nenggiri hydro plant expected to commence operations in 2027. Additionally, TNB launched its Electron brand to deploy EV charging infrastructure, with 22 fast-charging points identified across Malaysia in 2022, while securing 240 MWp of rooftop solar PV capacity for customers like Universiti Putra Malaysia and S P Setia. To support this transition, TNB established the Sustainability and Energy Transition Council (SETC) in Financial Year 2022, which combines the roles of former sustainability and energy transition committees to oversee strategic direction and implementation. The company is also expanding internationally through its New Energy Division (NED), which acquired CEI UK Ltd and added 97.3 MW of onshore wind capacity in the UK, while targeting 360 MW share by 2030 and 800 MW capacity by 2050 in ASEAN markets.",
