    "strategy": {
      "summary": "The EBRD's decarbonization strategy in 2022 centered on three interconnected pillars: full Paris Agreement alignment, integrated climate adaptation, and expanded green finance instruments. The bank achieved full Paris alignment for all new activities as of end-2022, implementing a rigorous methodology that categorizes investments into three types (directly financed, indirectly financed, and other investment types) with specific criteria. For directly financed projects, four mandatory conditions must be met: consistency with long-term low-carbon development, low likelihood of carbon lock-in, identification of physical climate risks, and ensuring activities don't undermine climate resilience. For indirectly financed investments, the EBRD transitioned from a 'transaction-based' to a 'counterparty-based' approach, requiring financial intermediaries to commit to aligning their financial flows with Paris Agreement goals and preparing transition plans within two years of signing financing agreements.\n\nTo operationalize this strategy, the EBRD developed a Climate Adaptation Action Plan (CAAP) in 2022 to catalyze transformation over three years. The CAAP prioritizes mainstreaming climate adaptation across operations, collaborating with partners to implement priority investments, and increasing adaptation investments through both direct and indirect finance channels. The bank significantly expanded its green finance instruments, particularly Climate Resilience Bonds (CRBs), which fund the Climate Resilience Project Portfolio (CRPP) targeting climate resilience infrastructure, business operations, and agricultural systems. By the end of 2022, the CRPP had €1.49 billion in operating assets and €1.51 billion in undrawn commitments across 81 projects.\n\nThe EBRD's 2022 decarbonization efforts yielded measurable impacts, with projects expected to reduce CO₂ emissions by 11,141 kilotonnes per year, save 58.4 million GJ of primary energy, and install 4,652 MW of renewable energy capacity. The bank implemented a new monitoring, reporting, and verification (MRV) approach to track actual project impacts. Specific transition projects included supporting the decommissioning of coal-fired combined heat and power plants in Kazakhstan and converting them to modern natural gas plants, which demonstrated alignment with a net-zero heating pathway by 2060. The EBRD also launched its Corporate Climate Governance Facility to help clients assess physical climate risks and develop transition plans.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across all provided materials. Strategy focuses on both mitigation and adaptation with specific implementation frameworks. Detailed metrics and concrete project examples provided. Strategy integrates financial instruments with operational implementation.",
      "status": null
    },
