    "strategy": {
      "summary": "H&M Group's 2022 decarbonization strategy centers on science-based targets and concrete actions across its value chain. The company aims to reduce absolute scope 1, 2 and 3 greenhouse gas emissions by 56% by 2030 (against a 2019 baseline) and achieve net-zero by 2040, with these goals verified by the Science Based Targets initiative. Their approach focuses on phasing out fossil fuels, particularly coal, from both operations and supply chain, while simultaneously scaling renewable energy adoption and circular business models. In 2022, they implemented a strict policy to stop onboarding new supplier factories with on-site coal boilers, resulting in a significant reduction in coal boiler usage from 91% to 70% among reporting suppliers. The company also made substantial financial commitments to decarbonization, spending approximately SEK 2.6 billion in 2022 on initiatives including energy efficiency investments in stores and supporting suppliers to transition away from fossil fuels.\n\nTo accelerate their decarbonization efforts, H&M established the Green Fashion Initiative to help suppliers replace fossil fuels, with 17 approved projects as of January 2023 that have the potential to reduce annual emissions by 50,000 tonnes CO2e. The company secured renewable energy through long-term virtual power purchase agreements (VPPAs) in the UK, Sweden, and Spain, and began construction of a solar park in Sweden that will supply renewable electricity at a fixed price starting in 2023. Additionally, they issued a EUR 500 million sustainability-linked bond in 2021 to finance initiatives focused on recycled materials and GHG emissions reduction throughout their value chain. H&M is also implementing circular business models through resell, remake, care, and repair solutions, which help decouple financial growth from resource use while reducing emissions.\n\nSupporting these initiatives is a comprehensive governance framework that includes quarterly KPI updates to the CEO and CFO, and integration of climate risk analysis through TCFD recommendations. The company has developed specific mitigation measures for climate scenarios, including transparency in customer communication about sustainability and collaboration with suppliers to phase out fossil fuels. H&M's strategy recognizes that decarbonization is not only an environmental imperative but also a business opportunity, as demonstrated by their focus on developing new customer-facing circular business models and exploring revenue streams that complement traditional operations while reducing climate impact.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found with specific implementation details for 2022. Strategy includes concrete financial commitments (SEK 2.6 billion spent in 2022), specific initiatives (Green Fashion Initiative, VPPAs), and measurable progress (reduction in coal boiler usage from 91% to 70%). Strategy focuses on both operational emissions (Scope 1 & 2) and supply chain emissions (Scope 3).",
      "status": null
    },
