    "strategy": {
      "summary": "MISC Berhad's 2022 decarbonization strategy follows a dual time horizon approach, targeting both short-to-medium term (up to 2030) and long-term (2031-2050) goals to achieve net-zero GHG emissions by 2050. The strategy centers on transitioning the existing fleet to lower-carbon technologies while simultaneously developing zero-carbon solutions for the future. Key focus areas include fleet renewal with dual-fuel LNG vessels, operational efficiency improvements, and the development of new business models centered around renewable energy and waste-to-value chains. The company has set an interim target of reducing shipping operations' GHG intensity by 50% compared to 2008 by 2030, aligning with IMO's climate ambitions while exceeding them with a net-zero commitment by 2050.\n\nTo execute this strategy, MISC is implementing several concrete initiatives. The company deployed 11 dual-fuel LNG vessels by the end of 2022 and is progressing with the Castor Initiative, a joint development project with Samsung Heavy Industries, Lloyd's Register, and MAN Energy Solution to create commercially viable deep-sea zero-emission vessels (ZEVs) by 2030. The first dual-fuel ammonia-fueled zero-carbon emission vessel is expected to enter service by late 2025, with a second following in early 2026. Additionally, MISC has implemented cold ironing at its Galveston facility, providing shore power to reduce emissions by an estimated 68% in 2021 and 67% in 2022, while also deploying environmental performance monitoring solutions (EPMS) to track emissions across its floating offshore assets.\n\nSupporting these operational initiatives, MISC has established governance structures and talent development programs to drive its decarbonization journey. The company renamed its Board Governance and Risk Committee to the Board Sustainability & Risk Committee (BSRC) effective January 1, 2023, to prioritize sustainability in strategic decision-making. A dedicated Task Force on Carbon Capture, Utilization, and Storage (CCUS) was formed to explore innovative solutions, while comprehensive seafarer development programs, including the Oil to Gas Conversion Programme and Capability Development for Dynamic Position Shuttle Tankers, ensure the workforce is prepared for low-carbon operations. The company also signed a Memorandum of Understanding in 2022 for the development of two very large crude carriers using green ammonia as propulsion fuel, demonstrating its commitment to developing commercially viable zero-emission solutions.",
      "notes": "Comprehensive multi-page strategy section found across pages 20-21, 58-59, 72-81, 88-89, 92-93, and 148-149. Strategy focuses on both operational emissions (Scope 1 & 2) and value chain emissions (Scope 3), with specific implementation details, timelines, and concrete initiatives. The company provides detailed information about fleet renewal, technological innovation, and talent development programs to support decarbonization.",
      "status": null
    },
