    "strategy": {
      "summary": "OCBC's decarbonization strategy centers on becoming a regional leader in sustainable banking by 2026 through its 5-year Climate Strategy unveiled in 2021. The approach is structured around three key priorities: achieving net-zero emissions aligned with Paris Agreement goals, enabling low-carbon transition through sustainable financing and partnerships, and effectively managing climate-related risks. The strategy is implemented across four key drivers: customers, innovation and technology, operations, and policy and systems, supported by people development and strategic partnerships. This comprehensive framework positions OCBC to address both the opportunities and risks of the low-carbon transition while maintaining financial stability.\n\nTo operationalize this strategy, OCBC joined the Net-Zero Banking Alliance (NZBA) in October 2022, committing to reduce attributable greenhouse gas emissions from lending and operational activities, with specific 2030 targets to be set within 18 months of joining and a 2050 target. The bank has also strengthened its responsible financing policies, implementing an Enhanced Energy Policy that prohibits new financing for clients where more than 50% of their total power generation comes from coal-fired plants, and an Enhanced Mining & Metals Policy with similar restrictions for thermal coal mines. These policies build on their 2019 commitment to stop financing new coal-fired power plants. In 2022, OCBC delivered its first sustainability-linked loan to Dyna-Mac Engineering Services, structured to reduce the company's scope 1 and 2 carbon intensity by 25% within five years.\n\nThe bank is actively developing innovative solutions to support decarbonization, including launching the OCBC Sustainability Innovation Challenge to identify solutions for sustainability challenges in food production. OCBC also partnered with the National University of Singapore to study effective EV adoption strategies, aiming to help Singaporeans transition to electric vehicles by 2040. To build internal capacity, the bank rolled out 14 new sustainability-related curricula for employees in 2022, increasing the proportion of sustainability-related curricula to 15% from 6% in 2021. Additionally, OCBC is integrating climate risk management into its Responsible Financing Framework and conducting scenario analysis to assess transition and physical climate risks across its portfolio, with particular attention to carbon-intensive sectors like upstream oil and gas and coal mining.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across several pages (7, 13-18). Strategy includes specific implementation details and measurable actions. The bank has moved beyond high-level commitments to concrete policies, partnerships, and financial products. Strategy focuses on both operational decarbonization and enabling client transition to low-carbon economy.",
      "status": null
    },
