      "summary": "PETRONAS Chemicals Group's (PCG) decarbonization strategy for 2022 centers on implementing a structured pathway toward Net Zero Carbon Emissions (NZCE) by 2050, with clear interim targets of 20% reduction by 2030 and more than half by 2040. The company's approach is guided by the Task Force on Climate-Related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) recommendations across governance, risk management, strategy, and metrics. In 2022, PCG published its first TCFD standalone report, applying the four key TCFD recommendations to identify climate-related risks and opportunities affecting business operations both physically and financially. This report enables the integration of climate risk into existing risk management frameworks and provides transparency to stakeholders.\n\nThe company is executing a four-stage reduction pathway to achieve its NZCE 2050 goals. The first stage, Operational Optimisation, targets 15-25% emissions reduction through energy efficiency initiatives, flare and venting reduction, and continuous process optimization at manufacturing plants. This includes implementing Advanced Process Control (APC) and Real-Time Optimizer (RTO) systems. The second stage, Asset Decarbonisation, aims for 50-60% reduction through Carbon Capture, Utilisation & Storage (CCUS), electrification, and adoption of low carbon process technology. The third stage, Switching to Renewables, targets 15% reduction through solar panel installation at asset rooftops and other renewable energy sources. Finally, the Carbon Offset stage addresses the remaining 10% through nature-based solutions.\n\nPCG is also advancing low carbon transition opportunities across three key areas: Carbon Capture, Utilisation & Storage; Circular Economy through chemical recycling; and Sustainable, Low Carbon Products including bio-based and renewable-based products. The company has initiated detailed assessments of Scope 3 emissions sources to develop a comprehensive baseline across 15 categories, enhancing the accuracy of emissions tracking. Additionally, PCG is leveraging digitalization to track and optimize energy consumption in real-time, while integrating climate risks identification and GHG mitigation initiatives into their Corporate Risk Profile (CRP) to embed climate strategy within business management.",
