    "strategy": {
      "summary": "PETRONAS Chemicals Group's (PCG) decarbonization strategy for 2022 is centered around the Net Zero Carbon Emissions (NZCE) 2050 Roadmap, which establishes clear, measurable targets: 20% emissions reduction by 2030, more than half by 2040, and net zero by 2050. The company implemented a four-stage reduction pathway to achieve these goals, beginning with Operational Optimization (~15-25% reduction) through energy efficiency initiatives, flare and venting reduction, and implementation of Advanced Process Control (APC) and Real-Time Optimizer (RTO) systems at manufacturing plants. For Asset Decarbonisation (~50-60% reduction), PCG is actively deploying Carbon Capture, Utilisation & Storage (CCUS), electrification, and integrating low carbon process technologies into existing assets.\n\nTo further advance its decarbonization efforts, PCG is switching to renewables (15% reduction) through solar panel installations at asset rooftops and exploring other forms of renewable energy. The company is also assessing nature-based carbon removal solutions like afforestation for the remaining 10% reduction through carbon offset. PCG has published its first TCFD standalone report, applying the four key TCFD recommendations (Governance, Risk Management, Strategy, and Metrics & Target) to systematically manage climate-related risks and opportunities across the organization.\n\nThe company is evaluating multiple low carbon transition opportunities including carbon capture technologies, circular economy approaches through chemical recycling, and sustainable low carbon products such as bio-based and renewable-based products. PCG has initiated detailed assessment of Scope 3 emissions across 15 categories to establish baselines, with plans to align with Greenhouse Gas Protocol standards and ISO 14064-1:2018. The strategy is supported by digitalization for real-time energy consumption tracking and optimization, integration of climate risk management into the Corporate Risk Profile (CRP), and enhanced governance structures including the Board Sustainability and Risk Committee (BSRC) and Sustainability and Risk Management Committee (SRMC).",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across pages 5, 10, 16, 48, 49, and 50. Strategy focuses primarily on operational emissions with specific reduction pathway and technology investments. Implementation details provided for each stage of the decarbonization roadmap.",
      "status": null
    },
