    "strategy": {
      "summary": "Tenaga Nasional Berhad's (TNB) decarbonization strategy for 2022 is centered around its Energy Transition (ET) Plan, which serves as the guiding framework for transforming Malaysia's energy landscape from fossil-fuel based sources to low-carbon energy solutions. The company has established a clear pathway focused on four key priorities: accelerating decarbonization, expanding renewable energy portfolio, modernizing the grid infrastructure, and empowering customers in the energy transition. TNB's approach integrates sustainability across its operations through three strategic pillars: Energy Sources (transitioning to cleaner generation), Energy Vector (enhancing grid capabilities), and Energy Usage (improving efficiency and customer engagement). The company has set concrete targets including a 35% reduction in carbon emission intensity by 2035 and 7,000 MW of renewable energy installed capacity by 2030, with a long-term goal of 14,000 MW by 2050.\n\nTo execute this strategy, TNB is implementing several specific initiatives. The company is accelerating decarbonization through early retirement of coal plants, upgrading existing facilities with cleaner technology, and deploying clean energy technologies including Carbon Capture, Utilisation and Storage (CCUS), hydrogen fuel, and Small Modular Reactors. On the renewable front, TNB is expanding its solar and wind energy assets both domestically and internationally, while developing hydro projects such as the Nenggiri hydro plant expected to commence operations in 2027. The company is also modernizing its grid infrastructure through the Grid of the Future (GoTF) initiative, which aims to improve grid flexibility and enable higher integration of Variable Renewable Energy (VRE) and Distributed Energy Resources (DER), with a Smart Grid Index (SGI) target of 85% by 2025.\n\nSupporting these initiatives is a robust governance structure centered around the Sustainability and Energy Transition Council (SETC), established in 2022 to coordinate and oversee the Energy Transition Plan. The SETC, chaired by the CEO and composed of TNB's corporate senior management team, is responsible for aligning sustainability visions with overarching sustainability pillars and shaping energy transition outcomes. TNB is also leveraging strategic partnerships with Original Equipment Manufacturers and other industry players to accelerate green technology development and deployment. The company has adopted the Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) framework to address climate-related risks and opportunities, and is implementing a comprehensive climate scenario analysis to guide its decarbonization efforts across different transition pathways.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across multiple report sections. Strategy details specific initiatives with concrete targets and implementation approaches. Governance structure clearly defined with SETC as central coordinating body. Strategy focuses on both operational and transition risks with specific management approaches outlined.",
      "status": null
    },
