      "summary": "Westports' decarbonization strategy for 2022 centers on a comprehensive approach targeting operational emissions through electrification, energy efficiency, and carbon pricing mechanisms, with a commitment to achieve net zero carbon emissions by 2050. The company has implemented an internal carbon price of RM15 per tonne to integrate climate considerations into long-term economic decisions, while focusing on electrifying key port equipment including RTG cranes, terminal trucks, and tugboats. Westports is addressing transition risks through its Risk Management Framework, incorporating detailed climate change assessments to guide strategic responses to regulatory and market shifts, and actively monitoring energy intensity across STS cranes, reefer containers, and port operational facilities.\n\nThe company is implementing specific decarbonization initiatives with concrete timelines, including the replacement of conventional RTG cranes with electric models starting in 2026, with plans to gradually convert all RTG engines to electric models from 2026 onwards. Currently, 53% of Westports' RTGs are conventional type, with the remainder being Hybrid and variable speed models. Westports is also investing in reducing emissions from tugboat fuel usage, aiming to use only renewable energy tugboats by 2050, and researching terminal truck electrification starting from 2024. Operational improvements include reducing terminal tractor futile trips, which contributes to emissions reduction, and supporting clean energy use through technical innovations to reduce fossil fuel reliance.\n\nTo support these efforts, Westports has joined the Malaysian chapter of the World Economic Forum's Climate Governance Initiative to acquire practical skills for climate transition strategy. The company is incorporating DHI-Ekomar's detailed climate change assessment into its Enterprise Risk Management framework to identify appropriate response strategies. Westports' internal carbon price objectives framework guides actions to navigate GHG regulations, drive energy efficiency, and promote low-carbon investments across operations, while also considering stakeholders' expectations and changing internal behavior toward sustainability.",
