    "strategy": {
      "summary": "CIMB's decarbonization strategy for 2024 centers on a structured financing approach to support the transition to a low-carbon economy across Southeast Asia, with particular focus on Malaysia. The bank has exceeded its RM100 billion sustainable finance target across 2021-2024, mobilizing RM117 billion total with RM31.1 billion specifically in 2024. Their approach prioritizes financing the green transition while aligning with national and regional climate goals, with a strategic focus on directing capital toward climate-aligned sectors while considering transition pathways for high-emitting industries. The bank has refined its financing frameworks to guide this allocation, with 48.7% of corporate client financing (RM8.6 billion total) dedicated to sustainability-linked financing and 75.7% of commercial client financing (RM7.7 billion total) allocated to sustainability-themed projects.\n\nTo operationalize this strategy, CIMB launched several concrete initiatives in 2024. The bank became the first in Malaysia to enable contactless credit or debit card payments for EV charging, removing barriers to electric vehicle adoption. Most significantly, CIMB introduced SMEBizReady in March 2024, a comprehensive program supporting 496 Malaysian SMEs with RM992.9 million in value-based financing. This program provides specific facilities including the High Tech and Green Facility (HTG), Low Carbon Transition Facility (LCTF), and Agrofood Facility (AF), all offered at preferential rates in partnership with Bank Negara Malaysia. Through these facilities, CIMB directly funded SME investments in renewable energy, low-carbon building materials, energy-efficient solutions, and circular economy initiatives. The bank also anticipates that national priority projects like large-scale solar projects under Malaysia's National Energy Transition Roadmap (NETR) will further drive their decarbonization financing.\n\nCIMB has established multiple strategic partnerships to enhance their decarbonization impact. The bank partnered with more than 10 business chambers and associations, including the Federation of Malaysian Manufacturers for Mission-Based Project 2.1 under the New Industrial Master Plan 2030. CIMB Islamic was selected as the strategic partner for establishing 3,000 smart factories across Malaysia and is collaborating with the Selangor Human Resource Development Centre to pioneer smart manufacturing innovation. Additionally, the bank is supporting SMEs through facilities like the SME Automation and Digitalisation Facility (ADF) to embrace digitalization and green technologies, recognizing that many SMEs face barriers in the transition to lower-carbon business models. This multi-faceted approach demonstrates CIMB's commitment to facilitating the region's transition to a low-carbon economy through targeted financing and strategic partnerships.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across all images. Strategy focuses on financing mechanisms and specific SME support programs. Detailed implementation through specific facilities (HTG, LCTF) and partnerships with national bodies. Strategy is implementation-focused with concrete figures and program names. The bank connects its financing approach directly to national transition roadmaps.",
      "status": null
    },
