    "strategy": {
      "summary": "Fubon Financial's decarbonization strategy for 2024 centers on a comprehensive approach to transform its investment and loan portfolios while supporting clients' transition to low-carbon operations. The company has established clear phase-out timelines for high-carbon investments, with commitments to eliminate investments in coal-related sources by 2030 (for companies deriving more than 5% of revenue from coal) and unconventional oil and gas by 2040. This approach is guided by the 'Fubon Financial Holding Co., Ltd. and Subsidiaries Sustainable Finance Policy,' which serves as their highest guiding principle for promoting sustainable finance. The strategy includes concrete portfolio management measures such as capping investments in 'high' ESG risk assets and high carbon industries at 22% within active funds, targets that were achieved in 2024. Fubon has also established credit standards that restrict funding for five high-carbon industries—power generation, coal mining, cement, petrochemicals, and steel—except when the funds support low-carbon transition, green energy development, or carbon reduction efforts. To accelerate this transition, Fubon has set a goal of NT$2.9 trillion in green finance by 2030, having already exceeded NT$2.5 trillion in 2024 through low-carbon investments, green energy technology investments, green bonds, and green financing.\n\nFubon's strategy extends beyond portfolio management to active client engagement and capacity building. In 2024, the company organized an SBT (Science-Based Targets) engagement workshop with over 30 corporate customers, focusing on setting Science-Based Targets for carbon reduction. They also hosted 'SME Net-zero Emissions Workshops' for small and medium-sized enterprises, attended by 107 representatives from 72 companies. The company has implemented a three-tiered ESG assessment mechanism for investments, including pre-investment checks (establishing blacklists/exclusion lists), post-investment monitoring, and verification of foreign discretionary investment institutions' ESG practices. Additionally, Fubon has joined the Partnership for Biodiversity Accounting Financials (PBAF) to assess the impact of investments on biodiversity, demonstrating an integrated approach to environmental sustainability.\n\nTo support implementation, Fubon has established governance structures including an ESG assessment mechanism with three lines of defense, and has integrated decarbonization into its core business operations across all subsidiaries. The company tracks renewable energy consumption, using 16.56 million kWh in 2024 (accounting for 16.4% of total electricity consumption), and has set specific targets for renewable energy adoption. Fubon's strategy is validated through international recognition, including being named to the DJSI World Index for the eighth consecutive year and maintaining an 'AA' MSCI ESG Index rating. The company also actively supports clients through green financing options, with Fubon Insurance providing corporate clients with loss prevention services for green energy risks and supporting green energy transitions through premium discounts.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across pages 03, 07, 08, 09, 35, 36, 46, 47, and 110. Strategy includes specific phase-out timelines, portfolio management targets, client engagement programs, and governance mechanisms. Strategy focuses primarily on investment and loan portfolio decarbonization with clear implementation details and 2024 achievements. The company has established concrete metrics and timelines for decarbonization, with specific numerical targets and phase-out schedules.",
      "status": null
    },
