    "strategy": {
      "summary": "KPS Berhad’s 2024 decarbonization strategy centers on a group-wide carbon emissions intensity reduction plan targeting a 45% reduction and ultimate carbon neutrality by 2050. The approach integrates decarbonization into core business operations, strategic planning, and corporate culture, emphasizing data-driven decision-making and alignment with global standards. Key focus areas include optimizing energy use through energy-efficient technologies, reducing carbon output via cleaner production methods, preserving water resources, and minimizing waste to support circular economy principles. These efforts are structured around four interconnected environmental focus areas that collectively address operational resilience in a low-carbon economy. \n\nIn 2024, the company significantly expanded its carbon accounting transparency by broadening Scope 1 and 2 emissions reporting to include subsidiary operations in Malaysia, Indonesia, China, and Vietnam, adhering to country-specific guidelines such as Malaysia’s Grid Emission Factor and Indonesia’s GRK System. The company also plans to extend Scope 3 coverage to include upstream and downstream transportation and distribution emissions. To operationalize these efforts, KPS Berhad is transitioning subsidiary manufacturing facilities to enhance energy efficiency, reduce fossil fuel reliance, and adopt renewable energy sources like solar power, while implementing predictive analytics to monitor and mitigate climate-related disruptions across the value chain. \n\nGovernance plays a critical role, with the Sustainability Board Committee (SBC) overseeing decarbonization progress through biannual reviews, strategic integration into business plans, and revised Terms of Reference to align with the Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) and Bursa Securities’ sustainability reporting framework. The SBC ensures sustainability is embedded in decision-making across all levels, with sustainability performance linked to senior management compensation. By mapping initiatives to six capitals—Financial, Manufactured, Intellectual, Human, Social, and Natural—the company mitigates capital erosion risks while reinforcing long-term resilience for stakeholders.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across Chapters 4, 5, and 6. Strategy focuses on operational emissions (Scope 1/2) with plans to expand Scope 3. Concrete initiatives include carbon accounting expansion, technology-driven energy efficiency, and governance integration. Implementation details are specific to subsidiaries and regional operations.",
      "status": null
    },
