      "summary": "The company’s 2024 decarbonization strategy focuses on reducing operational emissions through energy efficiency, renewable energy expansion, and electrification, aligned with Malaysia’s COP28 commitment to cut GHG emissions by 45% by 2030 and achieve carbon neutrality by 2050. A core approach involves upgrading infrastructure and integrating cleaner energy sources while leveraging robust emissions accounting to track progress against science-based targets. \n\nIn FY2024, the company implemented concrete initiatives: it replaced 44 units of IE2 electric motors with high-efficiency IE3 models at its Perak plants, investing approximately RM0.6 million to reduce electricity consumption and energy waste. Concurrently, it expanded solar panel capacity by 850 kWp at the same site, investing RM1.4 million to increase renewable energy’s share in its energy mix, with a goal to add 2 MWp total solar capacity by 2026. To further reduce fossil fuel reliance, the company launched a pilot project for electric vehicle (EV) forklifts and is exploring replacing additional diesel-powered equipment with electric alternatives. \n\nTo ensure transparency and accuracy, the company adopted the GHG Protocol with DEFRA (2023) emission factors, expanding its emissions inventory to include methane and nitrous oxide while enhancing Scope 1 and 2 tracking. This enabled a 20.9% reduction in Scope 1 emissions from FY2023, driven by motor replacements, reduced non-renewable fuel reliance, and a 23.4% decrease in diesel consumption compared to FY2022. The strategy also includes expanding Scope 3 accounting to cover purchased goods and services across subsidiaries, reinforcing commitment to comprehensive carbon management.",
