    "strategy": {
      "summary": "Astra Agro’s 2023 decarbonization strategy is structured around integrating climate action into core business operations, risk management, and long-term resilience, aligned with Indonesia’s Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) targets under the Paris Agreement. The company prioritizes reducing greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions through four strategic pillars: business transformation, operational efficiency, risk management, and data-driven intelligence. Central to this approach are concrete initiatives aimed at decarbonizing operations while enhancing climate resilience, including short- and medium-term decarbonization targets, regenerative farming practices, and Nature-Based Solutions (NBS) for carbon offset. The strategy emphasizes transitioning from climate-vulnerable practices to sustainable, low-carbon operations across all business lines, with a focus on mitigating both physical and transition risks arising from climate change.\n\nSpecific initiatives drive Astra Agro’s decarbonization efforts. Flagship programs such as 'Fuel Smart' prioritize biodiesel adoption and fuel efficiency, while 'Reuse & Recycle' enhances renewable energy capacity in operations. Operationally, the company deploys methane capture units to reduce emissions from palm oil mill effluents, transitions from chemical to organic/biological fertilizers, and implements climate-smart pest management. Business strategy efforts include developing climate-resilient oil palm varieties, enforcing 'Zero Fire' and 'No Deforestation' policies to protect ecosystems, and restoring ecological quality through 'Rehabilitasi' initiatives that prioritize ecologically beneficial planting. These actions are complemented by regenerative farming practices and exploration of low-carbon technologies to future-proof operations.\n\nTo support implementation, Astra Agro integrates climate risk into its corporate risk management framework, leveraging third-party expertise from PT Deloitte Konsultan Indonesia for TCFD-aligned risk analysis. The company conducts scenario assessments using global frameworks like the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) and Network for Greening the Financial System (NGFS) to model physical risks (e.g., floods, droughts) and transition risks (e.g., policy shifts, technological changes). This data-driven approach informs emergency planning, adaptation strategies, and integration of climate considerations into business decision-making, ensuring resilience against climate disruptions while advancing the transition to a low-carbon economy.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found; Strategy focuses primarily on operational emissions (Scope 1/2) and climate resilience; Includes concrete initiatives (e.g., methane capture, biodiesel use, regenerative farming) with limited numerical decarbonization targets; Risk management and scenario analysis are central to implementation.",
      "status": null
    },
