    "strategy": {
      "summary": "BNI's decarbonization strategy centers on a comprehensive approach that integrates climate risk management with financial services to drive emissions reduction across both operational and financed activities. The bank has developed a climate-related framework encompassing governance, strategy, risk management, and target setting, with specific focus on reducing GHG emissions from financing activities. BNI has set clear targets to achieve Operational Net Zero Emissions (NZE) by 2028 and Financing NZE by 2060, with a strategic emphasis on supporting the energy transition through targeted financial products and sector-specific risk management approaches.\n\nThe bank has implemented several concrete initiatives to advance its decarbonization goals. BNI has developed a Sustainability Linked Loan program to incentivize debtors to implement ESG and transition to green projects, particularly in renewable energy, eco-friendly transportation, green buildings, waste management, and sustainable land use. The bank has allocated 77% of green bond yields to these priority sectors, with renewable energy receiving the largest share at 61.8%. BNI has also launched a Sustainability Linked Loan specifically to push debtors toward energy transition and has set a green portfolio target of 5.2% for 2023 (which exceeded with 13.6% realization). Additionally, BNI is actively financing the transition from steam-fired to gas-fired power plants, supporting new renewable energy projects including solar, water, and geothermal power plants, and running the Public Electric Vehicle Charging Stations (SPKLU) cooperation scheme to stimulate adoption of electric vehicles.\n\nTo support these initiatives, BNI has established robust governance structures and risk management frameworks. The ESG performance management is housed within the ESG Management Department under the Enterprise Management Division, with oversight from the ESG Sub-Committee comprising Board members and senior executives. BNI has built infrastructure for emission data collection across all offices and has identified high-emission sectors in its credit portfolio, applying Loan Exposure Limits (LEL) to manage associated risks. The bank has commenced Climate Risk Stress Tests (CRST) for high-risk sectors including coal, oil palm plantation, and pulp & paper, and has strengthened Risk Acceptance Criteria for 18 sectors. BNI is also developing a policy on climate-related financial risk management aligned with IFRS S2 standards, with the framework directly reporting to the Risk Management Director and ultimately to the ESG Sub-Committee.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found with detailed implementation framework. Strategy focuses comprehensively on both operational emissions and financed emissions. Includes specific targets, timelines, and quantitative metrics (e.g., 77% of green bonds allocated to specific sectors, 61.8% for renewable energy). Contains concrete initiatives with clear implementation details rather than just high-level commitments. Well-structured governance framework with defined reporting lines. Strategy is supported by specific financial products and risk management approaches.",
      "status": null
    },
