      "summary": "PT Gunung Raja Paksi Tbk's decarbonization strategy for 2023 centers on the implementation of their Net Zero Roadmap Decarbonization, which was officially launched in February 2023. This strategic framework is structured around five core pillars, with Energy Transition and Low-Carbon Solutions forming the central pillar for emissions reduction. The company's approach focuses on transitioning to environmentally friendly energy sources, improving energy efficiency, implementing emission control measures, and developing carbon offsetting initiatives. This strategy is integrated with their broader sustainability roadmap that spans short-term preparation (2022-2023), medium-term experience accumulation (2023-2024), and long-term excellence (beyond 2025), demonstrating a comprehensive timeline for decarbonization.\n\nIn 2023, GRP implemented several concrete initiatives to advance their decarbonization goals. The company inaugurated Phase 1 of their Rooftop Solar Power Plant (PLTS) project at their factory, installing solar panels with a 900 kWp capacity that can generate 1,239,068 kWh of electricity annually. They established a robust GHG data management system to track and reduce emission intensity across Scope 1 and 2 emissions, while also implementing corporate-wide GHG data collection and reporting procedures. The company began exploring climate risks and opportunities in line with TCFD requirements and participated in the Asia Steel Market 2023 event to share their decarbonization strategy and explore green financing schemes.\n\nSupporting these initiatives, GRP has aligned its decarbonization efforts with international standards including ResponsibleSteel™ and GRI standards. They are working toward obtaining ResponsibleSteel™ certification at both site and product levels to validate their commitment to climate change mitigation. The company has established specific 2030 targets to achieve GHG emissions reduction that limits global average temperature to well below 2°C above pre-industrial levels, with efforts to increase this limit to 1.5°C. These efforts are part of a comprehensive approach that integrates sustainability principles into all business aspects while maintaining transparency through regular reporting and stakeholder engagement.",
