    "strategy": {
      "summary": "CIMB Niaga's 2024 decarbonization strategy follows a science-based approach aligned with the Net Zero 2050 ambition, with specific interim targets for 2030. The bank has established a dedicated governance structure comprising the Sustainability Unit and Integrated & Climate Risk Unit to manage climate-related risks and opportunities. The strategy focuses on six carbon-intensive sectors (thermal coal mining, cement, palm oil, power, oil and gas, and real estate), with tailored decarbonization targets for each sector based on the Net Zero Strategy & Climate Risk Management project launched in 2023. As part of the Net Zero Banking Alliance, the bank has committed to achieving net zero GHG emissions across Scope 1, 2, and 3 by 2050, with interim targets for Scope 1 and 2 by 2030.\n\nThe bank has implemented several concrete initiatives to reduce emissions, including an Internal Carbon Pricing (ICP) framework that assigns economic value to GHG emissions. Through this framework, they've purchased 11,284 MWh of Renewable Energy Certificates (RECs) in 2024, equivalent to 9,817.08 tons of CO2 reduction, and acquired 7,000 technology-based renewable energy carbon units. They've also installed solar panels at their Bintaro Head Office, which became operational in March 2024. Sector-specific actions include phasing out coal financing by 2040 in the power sector, implementing 'No Deforestation, No Peat, No Exploitation' commitments in palm oil, and supporting business diversification for oil and gas debtors toward renewable energy.\n\nTo support clients' transition, CIMB Niaga has launched gender-responsive financing programs like the 'Kartini Loan' for women entrepreneurs and integrated sustainability into risk management through Sustainability Due Diligence (SDD) mechanisms. The bank has already reduced Scope 1 and 2 GHG emissions by more than 12,500 tonnes of CO2 equivalent (32% reduction) compared to 2019 baseline, exceeding their initial target of 25%. They've also submitted Climate Scenario Analysis (CSA) reports to the Financial Services Authority (OJK) and early adopted International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS) S1 and S2 in their Sustainability Report to enhance transparency.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found across pages 9, 18, 19, 35, 40, 42, 73, 124, 174, and 217. Strategy includes specific sectoral targets, concrete implementation details, and measurable outcomes. The bank has already achieved significant emission reductions (32% for Scope 1 and 2) exceeding initial targets. Comprehensive governance structure with dedicated units supports the strategy.",
      "status": null
    },
