    "strategy": {
      "summary": "WIKA's decarbonization strategy for 2024 is systematically integrated into its business operations through a comprehensive ESG framework, with specific focus on renewable energy transition and low carbon solutions. The company has committed to conducting carbon emission baselines and preparing a decarbonization roadmap to reduce operational impacts on the environment, as identified in their 'Room for Improvement' section. This strategy aligns with national policies, particularly the National Energy Policy (KEN) which targets 23% renewable energy by 2025. WIKA's approach is structured around three main pillars: renewable energy adoption, operational efficiency, and responsible environmental management, with specific initiatives designed to address both Scope 1 and 2 emissions.\n\nThe company is implementing several concrete decarbonization initiatives across multiple business areas. WIKA has developed innovative energy-efficient technologies including Electric Water Heater, Smart Water Heater, Aircon Water Heater, and Heat Pump Water Heater, all designed to support carbon emission reduction through optimized energy consumption. The company is actively developing renewable energy projects such as solar panel installations and Carbon Capture, Utilization, and Storage (CCUS) technologies, while also advancing sustainable mobility through GESITS (an electric motorcycle) and Wika Battery for energy storage. Additionally, WIKA is optimizing its experience in energy infrastructure projects, including work on the Cilacap Coal Fired Power Plant and Grati Combined Cycle Power Plant, while focusing on floating solar power projects and geothermal development to accelerate the energy transition.\n\nTo support these initiatives, WIKA has established specific environmental policies including the Biodiversity and Land Restoration Policy (No. Doc. WIKA TFO-KP-01.01) and the HSE Policy (No. Doc. WIKA-HSE-KP-01.01), which emphasizes protecting the environment from project impacts. The company is collaborating with government initiatives to access sustainable financing and pro-energy transition regulations, while developing human capital in the EPCP (Engineering, Procurement, Construction, and Project) field to strengthen decarbonization capabilities. WIKA's decarbonization strategy is further supported by their commitment to waste management, emissions reduction, and biodiversity conservation, demonstrating a systematic approach to reducing environmental impact across all operational aspects.",
      "notes": "Multi-page strategy section found with concrete implementation details. Strategy focuses on operational emissions reduction through specific technology development and renewable energy projects. Includes measurable targets (23% renewable energy by 2025) and specific policy documentation. Strategy is integrated into business operations rather than being a standalone initiative.",
      "status": null
    },
